<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">scribe</ansi>

Scribe is a caster skill (ranks 1 to 4) that reads the hidden affixes of
Rare and better gear (<ansi fg="command">help identify</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Who has it</ansi>

  Wizards and clerics train it at a trainer with training points, like
  any skill (<ansi fg="command">train scribe</ansi>). The Magic Academy in Frostfang teaches it.
  A companion who is a wizard or cleric can learn it after joining:
  <ansi fg="command">company train [member] scribe</ansi> spends the companion's own points
  (<ansi fg="command">help company-train</ansi>). Some caster recruits already know it, show it on
  their card, and cost more.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What each rank reads</ansi>

  Rank 1  Rare     Rank 2  Epic     Rank 3  Legendary and Set
  Rank 4  also shows each affix's tier and range, and where the item
          came from (<ansi fg="command">look [item]</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">scribe</ansi> show them)

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">At camp</ansi>

When a camp rest finishes, your company's best Scribe present reads every
unidentified item the company carries that its rank covers, for no mana
(the rest refills it anyway). The company's best Scribe is the highest
rank with you; a tie goes to you, then to the lowest companion number.
It names who read. <ansi fg="command">autoskill scribe off</ansi> turns this off.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">In the field</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">scribe</ansi>                 what is waiting to be read, and who can read it
  <ansi fg="command">scribe [item]</ansi>          your best able Scribe reads one item
  <ansi fg="command">scribe [member] [item]</ansi> a named companion Scribe reads it

It costs mana: 8 for a Rare, 15 for an Epic, 25 for a Legendary or Set,
taken from the Scribe who reads. Mana only comes back from resting and
draughts, so reading in the field competes with healing and spells.
Nothing is read in battle.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help identify</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help rarity</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help affixes</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help skills</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help autoskill</ansi>.
